1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:04,086
A query and three candidate passages

2
00:00:04,106 --> 00:00:05,606
already have vectors.

3
00:00:05,781 --> 00:00:08,299
We compare them, and A comes first.

4
00:00:08,474 --> 00:00:11,057
Compare the very same vectors another way,

5
00:00:11,206 --> 00:00:12,719
and B comes first.

6
00:00:12,881 --> 00:00:14,523
No passage changed.

7
00:00:14,685 --> 00:00:17,010
No new relevance judgment arrived.

8
00:00:17,160 --> 00:00:19,343
What changed? Make a prediction.

9
00:00:21,776 --> 00:00:24,095
The numerical comparison rule changed.

10
00:00:24,257 --> 00:00:27,111
A dense encoder supplies representations,

11
00:00:27,131 --> 00:00:29,353
but the search system still needs a rule

12
00:00:29,373 --> 00:00:30,525
for comparing them.

13
00:00:30,675 --> 00:00:33,631
Cosine similarity and unnormalised dot

14
00:00:33,651 --> 00:00:35,744
product can give different leaders.

15
00:00:36,651 --> 00:00:39,258
A conventional bi-encoder represents each

16
00:00:39,278 --> 00:00:41,990
passage independently of the incoming

17
00:00:42,010 --> 00:00:44,284
query. Its passage vector can be

18
00:00:44,304 --> 00:00:46,925
calculated in advance and stored.

19
00:00:47,100 --> 00:00:49,889
When a query arrives, the system encodes

20
00:00:49,909 --> 00:00:52,338
that query and compares its vector with

21
00:00:52,358 --> 00:00:53,497
the stored ones.

22
00:00:54,034 --> 00:00:56,318
Query and passage encoders can share

23
00:00:56,338 --> 00:00:58,972
weights or use distinct parameters,

24
00:00:59,160 --> 00:01:01,666
normally trained together for retrieval.

25
00:01:01,841 --> 00:01:04,488
Our drawable space has just two anonymous

26
00:01:04,508 --> 00:01:06,718
coordinates. Real embeddings can have

27
00:01:06,738 --> 00:01:09,746
hundreds or thousands; these numbers are a

28
00:01:09,766 --> 00:01:12,761
declared toy, not model measurements.

29
00:01:13,658 --> 00:01:16,188
Think of each vector as an arrow from the

30
00:01:16,208 --> 00:01:18,972
same origin. Cosine compares the angle

31
00:01:18,992 --> 00:01:21,111
between arrows and divides out both

32
00:01:21,131 --> 00:01:23,701
lengths. A smaller angle means better

33
00:01:23,721 --> 00:01:26,034
alignment. Stretching an arrow in the same

34
00:01:26,054 --> 00:01:29,319
direction leaves its cosine unchanged.

35
00:01:29,874 --> 00:01:31,515
Same direction gives one.

36
00:01:31,665 --> 00:01:33,487
A right angle gives zero.

37
00:01:33,649 --> 00:01:36,374
Beyond ninety degrees the value becomes

38
00:01:36,394 --> 00:01:39,415
negative, reaching minus one for opposite

39
00:01:39,435 --> 00:01:41,645
directions. These are mathematical

40
00:01:41,665 --> 00:01:44,892
landmarks, not percentages of relevance or

41
00:01:44,912 --> 00:01:45,794
truth.

42
00:01:46,690 --> 00:01:48,856
Dot product multiplies matching

43
00:01:48,876 --> 00:01:51,600
coordinates and adds the products.

44
00:01:51,775 --> 00:01:54,049
Our query is one, zero.

45
00:01:54,198 --> 00:01:57,232
A is zero point nine, zero point three.

46
00:01:57,381 --> 00:02:00,428
B is one, one. A is better aligned, but B

47
00:02:00,448 --> 00:02:01,433
is longer.

48
00:02:01,992 --> 00:02:04,260
Their cosine scores are approximately zero

49
00:02:04,280 --> 00:02:07,018
point nine four nine and zero point seven

50
00:02:07,038 --> 00:02:09,260
zero seven, so A leads.

51
00:02:09,422 --> 00:02:11,528
Their unnormalised dot products are zero

52
00:02:11,548 --> 00:02:14,453
point nine and one, so B leads.

53
00:02:14,628 --> 00:02:16,940
C has a negative score under both rules.

54
00:02:17,844 --> 00:02:20,034
Now normalise the query and every

55
00:02:20,054 --> 00:02:21,747
candidate to length one.

56
00:02:21,922 --> 00:02:24,660
Dot product and cosine become numerically

57
00:02:24,680 --> 00:02:27,752
identical, and A leads under both rules.

58
00:02:27,915 --> 00:02:30,575
Removing magnitude removed the source of

59
00:02:30,595 --> 00:02:31,567
disagreement.

60
00:02:32,127 --> 00:02:34,572
This is a mathematical comparison, not a

61
00:02:34,592 --> 00:02:36,428
recommendation to change a production

62
00:02:36,448 --> 00:02:38,257
model's settings casually.

63
00:02:38,420 --> 00:02:40,436
An encoder may have learnt to use

64
00:02:40,456 --> 00:02:43,232
magnitude. The comparison rule and

65
00:02:43,252 --> 00:02:45,900
normalisation should match its training

66
00:02:45,920 --> 00:02:46,634
setup.

67
00:02:47,536 --> 00:02:50,118
Add a third coordinate: x, y, z.

68
00:02:50,294 --> 00:02:51,433
Add hundreds more.

69
00:02:51,595 --> 00:02:53,972
Dot product still multiplies matching

70
00:02:53,992 --> 00:02:56,111
coordinate pairs and adds them.

71
00:02:56,286 --> 00:02:58,431
Cosine still divides by both vector

72
00:02:58,451 --> 00:03:01,266
lengths. Higher dimensionality does not

73
00:03:01,286 --> 00:03:03,559
require a new comparison idea.

74
00:03:04,135 --> 00:03:06,859
Our axes are anonymous toy coordinates,

75
00:03:06,995 --> 00:03:08,547
not named concepts.

76
00:03:08,722 --> 00:03:10,893
A flat diagram cannot show a

77
00:03:10,913 --> 00:03:13,199
high-dimensional representation directly.

78
00:03:13,348 --> 00:03:15,944
A vector score also depends on the model

79
00:03:15,964 --> 00:03:17,902
and the comparison rule that produced it.

80
00:03:18,794 --> 00:03:20,971
The two orderings tell us which candidates

81
00:03:20,991 --> 00:03:23,251
scored highest under each rule.

82
00:03:23,414 --> 00:03:25,430
Neither establishes whether a passage

83
00:03:25,450 --> 00:03:27,917
serves a reader's task, context or

84
00:03:27,937 --> 00:03:29,424
inclusion criteria.

85
00:03:29,586 --> 00:03:31,976
Scores from different models or searches

86
00:03:31,996 --> 00:03:33,496
need not be comparable.

87
00:03:34,054 --> 00:03:36,731
Ask which model, preprocessing,

88
00:03:36,751 --> 00:03:39,386
normalisation and similarity rule are used

89
00:03:39,406 --> 00:03:41,925
together. Then evaluate the results

90
00:03:41,945 --> 00:03:43,483
against your information need.

91
00:03:43,646 --> 00:03:46,435
The next film keeps a rule fixed and asks

92
00:03:46,455 --> 00:03:48,780
a different question: which stored vectors

93
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:50,841
did the search actually examine?
